title: '[pallet-revive] Serve the ETH RPC from inside the node'

doc:
- audience: Node Operator
  description: |-
    `polkadot-omni-node` built with the new `eth-rpc` feature can serve the Ethereum
    JSON-RPC endpoint itself, instead of running a separate `eth-rpc` process that
    connects back over a loopback WebSocket. Pass `--eth-rpc` to enable it; the
    endpoint listens on localhost, on `--eth-rpc-port` (8545 by default), and stores
    its receipt database under `<data path>/eth-rpc`.

    The feature is experimental. The transport between the endpoint and the node is
    still being reworked, so its shape may change.

- audience: Node Dev
  description: |-
    `pallet-revive-eth-rpc` gains `in_process::InProcessRpcClient`, a `subxt`
    `RpcClientT` that dispatches JSON-RPC calls into a node's in-memory `RpcModule`
    rather than over a socket, and `service::start_embedded`, which builds the ETH
    RPC client on top of it and spawns the server on an existing `TaskManager`.

    Requests are still serialized to JSON and their SCALE payloads still decoded by
    subxt; only the transport changes. Skipping that work needs subxt's `Backend`
    trait, which is sealed today.

    `revive-dev-runtime` now depends on the individual `polkadot-sdk` crates instead
    of the umbrella crate. The umbrella re-exports `polkadot-omni-node-lib`, which
    made a node depending on `pallet-revive-eth-rpc` (which build-depends on the dev
    runtime to generate its subxt metadata) a cyclic package dependency.

    `pallet-revive-eth-rpc` gains `try-runtime` and `runtime-benchmarks` features that
    forward to the dependencies gating trait items on them. Without them a node taking
    the crate as a dependency cannot be built with `--all-features`.

crates:
- name: pallet-revive-eth-rpc
  bump: minor
- name: polkadot-omni-node-lib
  bump: minor
- name: polkadot-omni-node
  bump: patch
- name: revive-dev-runtime
  bump: minor
- name: revive-dev-node
  bump: patch
